<figure class="release-boundary-comparison">
  <div class="release-boundary-comparison__columns">
    <section class="release-boundary-comparison__local">
      <header><span>Local confidence</span><strong>Does the repository work here?</strong></header>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Source tests</b><small>Behavior is correct in the monorepo.</small></li>
        <li><b>Warm dependencies</b><small>Local output and tooling may hide assumptions.</small></li>
        <li><b>Workspace examples</b><small>Packages resolve directly from source.</small></li>
      </ul>
    </section>
    <div class="release-boundary-comparison__gap" aria-hidden="true"><span>release boundary</span><i>→</i></div>
    <section class="release-boundary-comparison__consumer">
      <header><span>Consumer confidence</span><strong>Can a user succeed with the published package?</strong></header>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Packed artifact</b><small>Tarball contents and transformed dependencies are correct.</small></li>
        <li><b>Fresh install</b><small>The package resolves without the monorepo's help.</small></li>
        <li><b>Strict compiler and browser</b><small>README imports, types, CSS, and runtime behavior work together.</small></li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </div>
  <figcaption>Repository checks provide useful evidence. Consumer checks prove the public package contract.</figcaption>
</figure>

